two weeks after young officers ousted  Burkina fussell's transitional president  in the country's second coup in less  than nine months Captain Ibraham Troy  was sworn in as president at a National  Assembly on Friday military religious  and Community leaders gathered to  formalize treorary's appointment  we need a committed patriot who is ready  to fight for the restoration of the  national territory  at the end of September 34 year old Troy  seized power promising to secure the  nation more effectively than his ousted  predecessor Lieutenant Colonel Paul  Henry sundogo damba who himself  overthrew the country's democratically  elected president in January people are  celebrating the appointment of terrori  as president they say because he's  fought on the ground and seen the  suffering of his people that he'll be  able to change the situation but many  here have little patience and say that  if they don't see results quickly he's  going to be ousted just like his  predecessor  Violence by armed groups has racked the  country for years killing thousands and  displacing some 2 million people some  locals who have fought alongside the  military say they are frustrated by the  lack of assistance and want the new  government to give them more weapons  we fight with our bulletproof jackets  and it's hard sometimes we spend seven  days in the bush we don't have enough  ammunition we have two magazines each  when there's a long attack we run out of  ammunition  many locals and Military are pushing  Troy to work more closely with Russia  instead of the country's former  colonizer France which has been fighting  armed groups in the region since 2013.  outside the meeting for the National  Assembly many were seen waving Russian  flags and denouncing France  our message is that if we want a good  collaboration it should be with Russia  France has been here for about 70 years  they are here they have Barracks here  we've never heard that a French soldier  died it's our soldiers who die but for  many it doesn't matter who Burkina  faso's new leaders work with as long as  they restore peace to their war-weekened  country  Sam mednick Al Jazeera wagadugu Burkina  Faso  [Music]
 

